    "body": "## Summary\n\nAdds **ADR-023: Skip-Subtree Optimization for Hover** — the fix for ADR-022's only remaining performance gap: wrapper-element hovers taking ~900ms because `cascade_subtree(el)` re-cascades every descendant of the hovered element.\n\n**Documentation only.** Implementation (3 commits) lands in a follow-up PR after this spec is reviewed and merged.\n\n## The refinement over ADR-022's Followup\n\nADR-022's Followup proposed a \"precompute hoverable set\" approach: cache which elements can ever match a `:hover` selector, then skip non-hoverable ones.\n\n**The ADR-023 analysis revealed a sharper insight** that makes the design smaller:\n\n> When you hover a wrapper `<div>`, **most of its descendants do not change style at all.** Per ADR-018's matcher, `:hover` is true for element X iff X is the hovered element OR X is an ancestor of the hovered element. The hovered `<div>`'s descendants are neither — they're descendants. So `a:hover` inside the wrapper does NOT match those `<a>` elements when the wrapper is hovered.\n\nThe correct re-cascade set on a wrapper-div hover is: **elements in the subtree that match some `:hover`-containing selector as the anchor, if they were the hovered element**. For UA's `a:hover`, that's the `<a>` elements in the subtree — ~50-100 out of ~5,000 descendants.\n\n`cascade_subtree(el)` currently cascades all 5,000. ADR-023 cascades only ~50-100. That's the whole win, and it's smaller than the original \"precompute hoverable set\" framing implied (precompute is a detail, not the design).\n\n## Three-level detection per stylesheet\n\n| Level | Detection | Behavior |\n|---|---|---|\n| **0** | No selector contains `:hover` | Skip hover work entirely (0ms) |\n| **1** | All `:hover` in anchor compound, no `:hover` elsewhere | Precompute `is_hover_sensitive`; on hover, cascade only flagged elements |\n| **2** | Any `:hover` in non-anchor compound (`div:hover > a`) | Fall back to ADR-022's unconditional `cascade_subtree` |\n\nOverall = max over all selectors in all sheets. **Current stylesheets: UA has only `a:hover` (Level 1); Wikipedia has no `:hover` rules → overall Level 1.** All the win, none of the correctness risk.\n\nLevel 2 fallback preserves correctness for compound-`:hover` stylesheets (unchanged from ADR-022 — correct but slow). Documented as deferred. No current use case.\n\n## Why precompute is the right mechanism\n\nThe naive check \"does this element match `a:hover`?\" costs O(elements × rules) via `selector.matches()`. Running it at hover time to filter is ~50% of the cost of cascading — saving 2×, not 30-50×.\n\n**Precomputing at render time amortizes the check.** Cost: 7,871 elements × 1 hover rule = 0.08ms per render on Wikipedia. Stored in `std::unordered_set<const Element*>`. Every subsequent hover pays only the O(subtree) pointer-membership walk (~1ms on Wikipedia), plus cascading the ~50-100 matched elements (~8-20ms).\n\n## Correctness invariant extended\n\nADR-022's invariant — **cache after targeted restyle == cache after full cascade, byte-for-byte** — still holds. The precompute is deliberately **conservative**: over-cascading is safe (wasted work, correct result), under-cascading is a bug.\n\nLevel 1's anchor-compound check is exact (not a heuristic in the algorithm — the underlying assumption is derived from ADR-018's matcher semantics and verifiable). Level 2 fallback is the maximally conservative case. Tested via extended cache-equality test in Commit 2.\n\n## Reviewer checklist — four decisions\n\n- [ ] **Level 1 detection assumption is sound.** Derivation: `:hover` in anchor compound of `S` means S's `:hover` tests the element being styled. So X can newly match S only if X is the hovered element, or an ancestor of it is. The precompute handles X-is-hovered; the walk handles X-descends-from-hovered. Trace once and confirm.\n- [ ] **Wrapper-div hover still does a full O(subtree) walk** even though most descendants aren't cascaded. ~1ms overhead. Acceptable, or want the deferred prev_hovered_-aware filtering?\n- [ ] **Level 2 fallback is unchanged** — compound `:hover` remains ~900ms. Wikipedia doesn't trigger it. Comfortable with deferral?\n- [ ] **Precompute \"over-cascades\" in one specific case**: hovering a `<div>` walks all its descendants and re-cascades flagged `<a>` elements even though their style didn't actually change (they're not hovered; their ancestors are). ~50-100 unnecessary cascade calls. Cost-benefit OK?\n\n## Deferred\n\n- Level 2 (compound `:hover`) optimization — no current use case\n- `prev_hovered_`-aware filtering (would drop wrapper-hover from ~8-20ms to ~1ms) — per-element hovered-state tracking needed\n- Cross-hover-transition optimization — same complexity\n- `:hover:visited` / `:hover:link` precompute edge cases — not exercised by current stylesheets\n- **Release build measurements** — debug baseline is 900ms; release may be <50ms already (unknown). Noted as an unknown; ADR-023 will measure post-implementation.\n\n## Expected outcome\n\nWrapper-element hovers on Wikipedia: **~900ms → ~8-20ms** (measured in Commit 2, will go into the ADR's Implementation section as the actual number). Closes ADR-022's only remaining performance gap for the current stylesheet ecosystem.\n\nLink hovers: **unchanged** (~14-25ms) — no regression on the 99% case.\n\n## Test plan\n\nNo code touched, so no test changes. Will run `cmake --build build && ./build/tests/honeybee_tests` after merge to confirm `main` still clean (260 cases, 1119 assertions, unchanged).\n\nRefs #148\n",
